The prefix logic looks correct:
```c
int prefix_filename = filename_mode == 1 || (filename_mode != -1 && file_count > 1);
if (!prefix_filename && recursive && has_directory) {
    prefix_filename = 1;
}
```

With `-rh`, `filename_mode` should be -1, so the first condition is false. The second part `(filename_mode != -1 && file_count > 1)` is also false because `filename_mode == -1`. Then we check if `!prefix_filename && recursive && has_directory` - but since prefix_filename is already false from the first line, this should set it to true only if there's a directory.

Wait, that's wrong! The spec says:
> `-h` given: no prefix
> otherwise `-H` given: prefix
> otherwise two or more file operands: prefix
> otherwise `-r` given **and at least one operand is a directory**: prefix

So with `-rh`, we should have NO prefix. But my code sets `prefix_filename = 1` when there's a directory and `-r` is set, even if `-h` was also specified.

The issue is that I'm checking `!prefix_filename` after already setting it based on the first condition. Let me fix this:
